{#
  The agree / disagree form, at the bottom of an evaluation.

  Expects:
    evaluation      — the Evaluation being reviewed
    myReview        — this user's existing EvaluationReview, or null
    reviewQuestions — active ReviewQuestion rows for the reason dropdown

  ── Graceful degradation is the whole design here ──────────────────────────
  With JavaScript off this is three plain controls and a submit button. The
  reason dropdown is always visible and its help text says when it applies, so
  nothing is hidden behind a script and nothing is unreachable. The Stimulus
  controller (review_form) is a courtesy on top: it hides the dropdown while
  "agree" is selected, which is tidier but changes no outcome. Every rule it
  appears to enforce is re-checked in EvaluationReviewController, because a
  browser is not a place to keep rules.

  ── Why the verdict is radios and not two submit buttons ───────────────────
  Two buttons ("I agree" / "I disagree") would be fewer clicks, but they would
  also mean a comment typed before choosing gets attached to whichever button
  the evaluator eventually presses — and, worse, that "I disagree" submits
  without a reason and bounces back. Radios make the verdict a visible,
  changeable part of the form, which is also what makes editing an existing
  review show you what you previously said.
#}
<section id="review" class="review-form" aria-labelledby="review-form-heading">
  <h2 id="review-form-heading">
    {{ myReview ? 'Your review' : 'Do you agree with this evaluation?' }}
  </h2>

  {% if myReview %}
    {#
      Editing, not adding. Saying so explicitly matters because the form is
      pre-filled: without this line a returning evaluator sees their old answer
      selected and cannot tell whether it is a saved verdict or a stray default.
    #}
    <p class="muted">
      You recorded this on {{ myReview.recordedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}{% if myReview.wasEdited %} (edited){% endif %}.
      Submitting again replaces it — each evaluator holds one review per evaluation.
    </p>
  {% endif %}

  <form method="post"
        action="{{ path('lumina_ui_evaluation_review', {id: evaluation.id}) }}"
        data-controller="review-form">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('review-' ~ evaluation.id) }}">

    {#
      A <fieldset> with a <legend> is what groups radios for assistive tech: it
      is how "I agree with this evaluation" gets announced as an answer to
      "Your verdict" rather than as a stray checkbox floating in the page.
    #}
    <fieldset class="review-verdict">
      <legend>Your verdict</legend>

      {% for verdict in ['agree', 'disagree'] %}
        {% set checked = myReview and myReview.verdict.value == verdict %}
        <label class="review-option" for="verdict-{{ verdict }}">
          <input type="radio"
                 id="verdict-{{ verdict }}"
                 name="verdict"
                 value="{{ verdict }}"
                 {{ checked ? 'checked' : '' }}
                 required
                 data-review-form-target="verdict"
                 data-action="change->review-form#toggleReason">
          {# The full first-person sentence, straight off the enum, so the
             wording is identical here and in the comment list below. #}
          <span>{{ verdict == 'agree' ? 'I agree with this evaluation' : 'I disagree with this evaluation' }}</span>
        </label>
      {% endfor %}
    </fieldset>

    {#
      The reason. `hidden` is NOT set here — the server-rendered default is
      visible, which is what makes the form usable without JavaScript. The
      Stimulus controller hides it on connect when the state warrants it.
    #}
    <div class="field" data-review-form-target="reason">
      <label for="review-question">Why do you disagree?</label>
      <select id="review-question" name="question" aria-describedby="review-question-help">
        <option value="">Choose a reason…</option>
        {% for question in reviewQuestions %}
          <option value="{{ question.id }}"
                  {{ myReview and myReview.question and myReview.question.id == question.id ? 'selected' : '' }}>
            {{ question.displayLabel }}
          </option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
      <p id="review-question-help" class="muted field-help">
        Required when you disagree. Ignored if you agree.
        {#
          Descriptions are optional per reason and most will not have one, so
          this only renders when there is something to say. Shown for ALL of
          them rather than only the selected one because, with no JavaScript,
          there is no "selected one" until the form is submitted.
        #}
      </p>
      {% set described = reviewQuestions|filter(q => q.description) %}
      {% if described is not empty %}
        <details class="review-reason-help">
          <summary>What these reasons mean</summary>
          <dl>
            {% for question in described %}
              <dt>{{ question.displayLabel }}</dt>
              <dd>{{ question.description }}</dd>
            {% endfor %}
          </dl>
        </details>
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label for="review-comments">Brief comments <span class="muted">(optional)</span></label>
      <textarea id="review-comments"
                name="comments"
                rows="3"
                maxlength="2000"
                aria-describedby="review-comments-help">{{ myReview ? myReview.comments : '' }}</textarea>
      <p id="review-comments-help" class="muted field-help">
        Shown to other evaluators at the bottom of this page, with your name.
      </p>
    </div>

    <button type="submit">{{ myReview ? 'Update my review' : 'Record my review' }}</button>
  </form>
</section>
